Date: Thu, 11 Nov 1993 11:58:33 -0500 (EST)
From: LEVY%BESSIE@venus.cis.yale.edu
Subject: Social Sciences in Academia (was Physics as Therapy)

Jamie Dinkelacker writes:

> The social/behavioral disciplines are plagued by a fundamental problem:
> absence of measurement rigor. Without this basis, it's nearly impossible to
> have agreement on premises, let alone be rigorous in derivations.

> These disciplines lack conventional standards that (1) identify key
> variables (e.g., no STP); (2) establish conventional means for ascribing
> symbols to phenomena (e.g., no consistent measurement instruments -- Likert
> scales or semantic differentials, fractionalization scales, ... just don't
> count); or (3) establish units of measure (e.g., no meter, gram, liter,
> coulomb, joule, ...).

Good points, but I think it's even worse than Jamie does.  After spending nearly
ten years as a student of linguistics -- probably the most rigorous of the social
sciences in terms of coherent theory and methodology -- I've come to believe that
the problem isn't something as straightforward as agreeing on a single set of
terms or measurement techniques.  The problem, I believe, is in applying a paradigm
from the physical sciences (hypothesize, measure, analyze, verify/falsify) to
systems (language, culture, the human psyche) that are far more complex than the
things that physicists and chemists study.  This isn't a value judgment about
physics, chemistry, or the social sciences; it's a statement of belief about the
nature of the systems being studied.

Even the best minds in the social sciences often exhibit a "Field of Dreams"
attitude toward their discipline -- "If we build it (the latest theory), they
(satisfying answers) will come."  Richard Feynman, ever the harsh critic of
soft, fuzzy thinking, called this "cargo cult science."  

What is/are the answer(s)?  First -- and this is going to bother a lot of people --
I think it's reasonable to hold social science research to some criterion of
(gasp!) real-world usefulness, by which I mean that there's someone (other than
the taxpayers) who have a use for your research and will pay you for it.  The
classic example of this for me is speech recognition -- people originally thought
it would be an easy matter to get computers that could converse with human beings,
but the problem turned out to be so difficult that it spawned a new field driven
by a single goal.  A good deal of the research in this field had very little to do
with traditional academic linguistics or psychology, and it revealed a lot of things
about speech and language that no one had ever had to consider before.

Second, I think that taking a *synthetic* rather than *analytical* approach holds
a good deal of promise.  Instead of trying to break down the world of human behavior
into units which can account for only a small fraction of what interests us, why not
try to synthesize something simple to begin with, and see whether it can be made to 
evolve into something more interesting?  Then, we don't need a "theory of behavior"
or a "theory of languge".  The system itself becomes the theory -- things are the way
they are because they evolved that way, to paraphrase Ilya Prigogine.

-- Simon!



