Subject: Bi- vs. Semi-

> I'm also embarrassed--but not too proud--to ask how people out there
> remember whether "bi-weekly" means "twice a week" or "every two
> weeks"? Anton? Isaac? Any good mnemonic aids to break my constant
> mental block over this?
> 
> (Hey, I just consulted my OED II, and it states that _either_ usage is
> correct, and hence terribly ambiguous; it suggests "semi-weekly" for
> the "every two weeks" usage. Maybe that's why I can never keep these
> straight. I never knew both contradictory usages were normal.)

Well, as my wife, who was a linguistics major, would be quick to remind
me, one can't argue too vehemently against the idea that word meaning
can drift with usage, even incorrect usage.

However, as a mnemonic for the correct usage, think of the word
(bi-weekly, semi-weekly) as denoting the duration between repetitions
of the event (e.g. IRC gatherings). If there are two weeks between
repetitions, it is a BI-weekly occurrance. If there is only half a week
between repetitions, it's SEMI-weekly.  The quaint roots semi-, hemi-,
and demi-, from latin, french, and greek, respectively,  mean "half of"
(e.g. "hemisphere" and "demitasse")

